# Exam track — Theme: Summer — Sub-lesson 3: Talking About It
# @question sentences: the examiner asks in Luxembourgish, the learner
# assembles the Luxembourgish answer (always en→lu).
#
# TWO SPEAKERS share this theme — Sergii and his wife Marie — and several
# examiner questions therefore carry two different answers, both genuine and
# both worth rehearsing (they moved in different months, their favourite
# seasons differ, their birthdays fall in different seasons). These are their
# real interview answers: do not "improve", merge or genericize them in a later
# editing pass, and do not resolve the two answers into one.
#
# Key personal facts. Sergii: summer means warm weather and holiday time, three
# months (June/July/August); 20–25 degrees is his range, because that is when he
# can walk the dog; favourite season is winter for the snow and Christmas, and
# spring for his birthday; least favourite is spring and late autumn because the
# forest is wet and dirty; as a child he went to the sea because his parents
# thought it good for children, loved motorboats (only two or three rides, they
# were expensive), and spent summers at his grandparents' house outside the city
# by the river; this summer he stayed in Luxembourg with two short trips to
# France and Italy, ran and swam a lot, saw castles and forests; went to the
# Schueberfouer with work colleagues, a few rides and lunch; bought an air
# conditioner at a market in France against the heat; has a small garden in its
# first year with a single hydrangea that moved house with him and has lived
# with him for six years; the Dolomites were the most beautiful landscape he has
# seen; Ukraine gets up to forty degrees and is largely landlocked, so its
# climate is warm and too dry.
# Marie: from Ukraine, six years in Luxembourg, moved from Italy in November,
# in autumn — cold, wet, a big adjustment, she had to buy a warm coat; birthday
# in summer, but weather-wise she prefers mountain winter with snow and sun, not
# rain-only winter; likes Luxembourg's summer for the crowded streets and
# terraces, the events and the wine festivals; this summer had three heatwaves,
# so she stayed home, closed every window and used the air conditioning; spends
# a lot of time outdoors on terraces, coffee in the garden, walking in good
# weather; August in France, June and July in Luxembourg; this summer they moved
# into a new flat, so they stayed and drove around looking at castles; she
# recommends the Little Switzerland to a tourist.
#
# LOD notes. Corrections applied to the spoken wording: Klimm -> d'Klimaanlag,
# recommendéieren -> recommandéieren, Éisterräich -> Éisträich, besichen (a
# person) -> besichtegen (a place), Liiblings- -> Liblings-. `Hortensien`,
# `Dolomitten` and `Nordfrankräich` are absent from LOD with no spelling
# suggestions (proper names and a borrowed plant name); they are kept as the
# learners said them and appear only inside sentences, never as @word entries.
#
# The @fill blocks carry frames that recur across any topic: well-causal,
# wann-conditional, obwuel-contrast, léiwer-wéi comparative, fir…ze purpose,
# dofir inversion, wärenddeems-temporal, säit-duration, datt-hedge, a frequency
# adverb, a number, a past participle, and a noun blank.

@lesson J1.03 "Talking About Summer"

@word d'Fro = the question
@word d'Äntwert = the answer
@word d'Meenung = the opinion
@word de Grond = the reason
@word d'Iddi = the idea
@word d'Erënnerung = the memory
@word d'Zäit = the time
@word d'Plaz = the place
@word d'Stad = the city
@word d'Haus = the house
@word d'Elteren = the parents
@word d'Kand = the child
@word d'Kanner = the children
@word d'Fra = the wife
@word de Mupp = the pooch
@word d'Aarbecht = the job
@word den Aarbechtskolleeg = the work colleague
@word de Weekend = the weekend
@word den Ausfluch = the outing
@word de Wanderwee = the hiking trail
@word de Trëppeltour = the walk
@word de Strand = the beach
@word d'Schwämm = the swimming pool
@word de Vëlo = the bike
@word d'Glace = the ice cream
@word d'Waasser = the water
@word de Bam = the tree
@word d'Wiss = the meadow
@word de Norden = the north
@word de Chrëschtdag = Christmas Day
@word de Mëtteg = the midday
@word moies = in the morning
@word owes = in the evening
@word dacks = often
@word heiansdo = sometimes
@word meeschtens = mostly
@word normalerweis = normally
@word léiwer = rather
@word dobaussen = outside
@word doheem = at home
@word ganz = very
@word richteg = really
@word stolz = proud
@word schwiereg = demanding
@word perfekt = perfect
@word nei = new
@word kuerz = short
@word gesinn = to see
@word fueren = to drive
@word lafen = to run
@word kucken = to look at
@word fannen = to find
@word mengen = to believe
@word vergläichen = to compare
@word recommandéieren = to recommend
@word genéissen = to enjoy
@word opstoen = to get up
@word erënneren = to remember
@word vergiessen = to forget
@word zwou = two (feminine)

# --- What summer means, and when it is ---

@sentence
@question Firwat hutt Dir dëst Theema gewielt?
@lu Ech hunn de Summer gär, och wann et net meng Liblingsjoreszäit ass.
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-lu Fro
@en I like the summer, even if it is not my favourite season.
@distractor-en winter
@distractor-en question

@sentence
@question Wéini ass de Summer?
@lu De Summer ass dräi Méint: Juni, Juli an August.
@distractor-lu zwielef
@distractor-lu Dezember
@en The summer is three months: June, July and August.
@distractor-en twelve
@distractor-en December

@sentence
@question Wat heescht Summer fir Iech?
@lu Fir mech ass de Summer eng Zäit fir Vakanz, wann ech an d'Bierger oder bei e Séi fuere kann.
@distractor-lu Aarbecht
@distractor-lu Stad
@en For me the summer is a time for holidays, when I can drive to the mountains or to a lake.
@distractor-en job
@distractor-en city

@sentence
@question Wat maacht Dir am léifsten am Summer?
@lu Meng Fra, eisen Hond an ech kënne vill spadséiere goen.
@distractor-lu Aarbechtskolleeg
@distractor-lu wéineg
@en My wife, our dog and I can go for a lot of walks.
@distractor-en colleague
@distractor-en little

@sentence
@question Wéi eng Temperature sinn ze waarm fir Iech?
@lu Zwanzeg bis fënnefanzwanzeg Grad sinn ideal, an alles driwwer ass ze waarm.
@distractor-lu véierzeg
@distractor-lu kal
@en Twenty to twenty-five degrees are ideal, and everything above that is too warm.
@distractor-en forty
@distractor-en cold

@sentence
@question Firwat sinn zwanzeg bis fënnefanzwanzeg Grad ideal?
@lu Bei där Temperatur kann ech mat mengem Mupp spadséiere goen.
@distractor-lu Schnéi
@distractor-lu Vëlo
@en At that temperature I can go for a walk with my pooch.
@distractor-en snow
@distractor-en bike

# --- Favourite and least favourite season ---

@sentence
@question Wat ass Är Liblingsjoreszäit a firwat?
@lu Meng Liblingsjoreszäit ass d'Fréijoer, well ech da mäi Gebuertsdag hunn.
@distractor-lu Hierscht
@distractor-lu Aarbecht
@en My favourite season is the spring, because that is when I have my birthday.
@distractor-en autumn
@distractor-en job

@sentence
